{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"959e4986413be74cd5e08b765b161fac2051494d68d5c34cc8b8342cb5a27dc8","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_9f83f9b7d1267d6b39b39ffcb9c7ede120b9f1c45d9eb3d1606ea715db0bd3ce","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a mathematically sound and efficient method for implementing equivariance to spatial transformations via warped convolutions. The theoretical derivation is clear, and the practical implementation is well-supported by ablation studies on two datasets. However, the experimental evaluation is limited by the small size of the Google Earth dataset, the lack of statistical significance testing, and the absence of comparisons to state-of-the-art deep learning baselines for the specific tasks.","paper_id":"BkmM8Dceg","paper_identity_sha256":"831cb16ec065ca85f1a7399379ad19e25f87613264232a7dfcee0daa9c8ec58d","prompt_identity_sha256":"3f7d9fb24e80642c68a20fb2a1feaad0c2853369451ff58f0142ef75724bf06f","raw_response_sha256":"21bd72e438d037a0c33246847ceb786a337be9a6001541f8afb0fcf17b5f55fe","request_identity_sha256":"215c9ece59e7dda8457e16ae79861a8c83fcb4eb68849ea0f139c331412ace19","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"20d9192a3432fe1d09390a0779d762a13612e42a9ad3c2ccf5c896b095c3cb41","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-015","SRC-016"],"review_text":"The proposed method is technically sound and mathematically consistent. Theorem 1 correctly derives that generalized convolution can be implemented as a standard convolution on a warped image, provided the transformation group has an additive parametrization and a pivot point exists. The proof is valid, and the practical simplification of learning the warped filter directly (ignoring the constant Jacobian) is a sound reparametrization. The requirement that transformations must commute for multi-parameter cases is correctly identified and respected in the examples provided.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-028","SRC-029","SRC-030","SRC-031","SRC-032"],"review_text":"The experimental design includes appropriate ablation studies to isolate the contribution of the warped convolution. The authors compare the proposed method against baselines with the warp removed (CNN+softargmax, STN+softargmax) and against non-equivariant variants (CNN+FC, STN+FC), which effectively validates the architectural claim. However, the Google Earth dataset is very small (697 vehicles), which limits the statistical power of the results. The face pose estimation on AFLW is more robust, but the baselines are limited to simple CNN/STN variants rather than state-of-the-art pose estimation methods.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-028","SRC-031","SRC-032"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient implementation details for reproduction, including network architectures, filter sizes, channel counts, pooling strategies, and training procedures (SGD, 20 epochs, L1 loss). The warp grids are described as being generated offline, and the specific transformations (scale/rotation, 3D yaw/pitch) are defined with equations. However, the paper does not specify the exact hyperparameters (learning rate, weight decay, etc.) used for the SGD optimizer, nor does it provide code availability, which may hinder exact reproduction.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-029","SRC-033"],"review_text":"The paper compares its method against relevant prior work in the context of generalized convolutions (e.g., Kanazawa et al., Cohen & Welling, Marcos et al.) and spatial transformer networks. The baselines in the experiments are designed to ablate the specific contribution of the warp, which is appropriate for validating the method. However, the paper does not compare against state-of-the-art deep learning methods for vehicle pose estimation or face pose estimation, which limits the assessment of its practical competitiveness in these specific tasks.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-027","SRC-030"],"review_text":"The paper reports mean errors on validation sets but does not provide confidence intervals, standard deviations, or statistical significance tests. Given the small size of the Google Earth dataset (697 samples), the reported improvements (e.g., 28.87 to 26.44 degrees) may not be statistically significant. The lack of variance reporting makes it difficult to assess the robustness of the results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"fbb7f8c13780a57e85cb02b0180a86b709715cd3c1651e451737d1a07abd78af","paper_id":"BkmM8Dceg","paper_ordinal":60,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"21bd72e438d037a0c33246847ceb786a337be9a6001541f8afb0fcf17b5f55fe","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BkmM8Dceg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"eafc8c934f171191f80cf01dc916d930a1ea383fe9e4339a510a1fd3c90cfe3d","request_identity_sha256":"215c9ece59e7dda8457e16ae79861a8c83fcb4eb68849ea0f139c331412ace19","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":984,"prompt_tokens":19226,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":20210},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"215c9ece59e7dda8457e16ae79861a8c83fcb4eb68849ea0f139c331412ace19","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}